-- title:
--   The maximum norm is dominated by the Euclidean norm
-- statement:
--   For every $x \in \mathbb{R}^d$,
--   $$\|x\|_\infty \;\le\; \|x\|_2,
--   \qquad\text{where}\quad \|x\|_\infty = \sup_i |x_i|
--   \quad\text{and}\quad \|x\|_2 = \Bigl(\sum_i x_i^2\Bigr)^{1/2}.$$
--
--   One half of the standard comparison of the two norms on $\mathbb{R}^d$; it is what converts a Euclidean estimate — a ball, a distance between points — into a statement about cubes, which are defined by the maximum norm.
--
--   **Formalization Note.** The ambient type is `EuclideanSpace ℝ (Fin d)`, whose native norm $\|\cdot\|$ is the $\ell^2$ norm; the maximum norm is the separately defined indexed supremum of the coordinates.
-- source:
--   https://github.com/dbenbenn/quadratic-forms-sobolev/blob/7a1a680db2124d46ce370c91fd450aa454edf491/QuadraticFormsSobolev/Cubes.lean#L46-L49

